{"page":1002,"blocks":[{"x":0.04849,"y":0.05257,"w":0.30808,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"988 Chapter 16: Integrals and Vector Fields"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.093,"w":0.37903,"h":0.01375,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Using Green’s Theorem to Evaluate Line Integrals"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.11764,"w":0.59312,"h":0.07419,"fs":0.01279,"n":5,"k":1,"t":"If we construct a closed curve C by piecing together a number of different curves end to end, the process of evaluating a line integral over C can be lengthy because there are so many different integrals to evaluate. If C bounds a region R to which Green’s Theorem applies, however, we can use Green’s Theorem to change the line integral around C into one double integral over R."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.21955,"w":0.28836,"h":0.01449,"fs":0.01407,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"EXAMPLE 4 Evaluate the line integral"},{"x":0.57567,"y":0.25513,"w":0.09495,"h":0.01728,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"xy dy - y2 dx,"},{"x":0.55607,"y":0.27045,"w":0.01791,"h":0.01952,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"F C"},{"x":0.32333,"y":0.29858,"w":0.51872,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"where C is the square cut from the first quadrant by the lines x = 1 and y = 1."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.32526,"w":0.59297,"h":0.02899,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"Solution We can use either form of Green’s Theorem to change the line integral into a double integral over the square, where C is the square’s boundary and R is its interior."},{"x":0.3315,"y":0.36307,"w":0.58485,"h":0.02956,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"1. With the Tangential Form Equation (3): Taking M = -y2 and N = xy gives the result:"},{"x":0.43312,"y":0.42138,"w":0.11917,"h":0.01727,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"-y2 dx + xy dy ="},{"x":0.58702,"y":0.42519,"w":0.13696,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"(y - (-2y)) dx dy ="},{"x":0.765,"y":0.42586,"w":0.06751,"h":0.02411,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"0 3y dx dy"},{"x":0.4107,"y":0.4367,"w":0.01791,"h":0.01952,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"F C"},{"x":0.56